{"id":1916,"date":"2026-08-25T17:48:10","date_gmt":"2026-08-25T17:48:10","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/?post_type=knowledgebase&#038;p=1916"},"modified":"2026-08-25T18:09:35","modified_gmt":"2026-08-25T18:09:35","slug":"cmyk-commercial-print-mode","status":"publish","type":"knowledgebase","link":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/db\/accessibility\/cmyk-commercial-print-mode\/","title":{"rendered":"CMYK Commercial Print Mode"},"content":{"rendered":"<div style=\"color: #2b2b2b;\">\n<h2 class=\"western\"><span style=\"color: #007670;\">CMYK Commercial Print Mode<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignright wp-image-1927\" src=\"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/CmykCommercialPrintMode.webp\" alt=\"CMYK Commercial Print Mode\" width=\"128\" height=\"133\" srcset=\"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/CmykCommercialPrintMode.webp 929w, http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/CmykCommercialPrintMode-288x300.webp 288w, http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/CmykCommercialPrintMode-768x799.webp 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 128px) 100vw, 128px\" \/><\/span><\/h2>\n<p class=\"western\">Professional printing services work in the <strong>CMYK colour model<\/strong> (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Key\/Black) rather than the RGB model used by screens. CMYK has a smaller colour gamut &#8211; it cannot reproduce certain vivid neon or electric colours that look brilliant on screen. A design that appears vibrant on your monitor can look noticeably duller when printed with physical ink.<\/p>\n<p class=\"western\">Open Publisher&#8217;s <strong>CMYK Soft-Proof mode<\/strong> lets you preview exactly how your colours will shift before sending your file to a commercial printer, so there are no surprises when the physical copies arrive.<\/p>\n<h3 class=\"western\"><span style=\"color: #007670;\">Enabling CMYK Mode<strong><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignright wp-image-1921\" src=\"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/Screenshot-2026-08-25-185013.png\" alt=\"Enabling CMYK Mode\" width=\"522\" height=\"133\" srcset=\"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/Screenshot-2026-08-25-185013.png 1451w, http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/Screenshot-2026-08-25-185013-300x76.png 300w, http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/Screenshot-2026-08-25-185013-1024x261.png 1024w, http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/Screenshot-2026-08-25-185013-768x196.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 522px) 100vw, 522px\" \/><\/strong><\/span><\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-size: small;\">Go to <strong>File &gt; Info<\/strong>.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/li>\n<li>\n<p align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-size: small;\">Find the <strong>Colour Mode<\/strong> toggle and switch from <strong>RGB<\/strong> to <strong>CMYK<\/strong>.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p class=\"western\">When CMYK mode is active, Open Publisher applies a custom <strong>SVG soft-proof filter<\/strong> across the entire canvas. Vivid, out-of-gamut colours &#8211; such as neon greens, electric blues, and hot pinks &#8211; are intelligently dulled to give you an accurate real-time preview of how the document will look when printed with physical ink.<\/p>\n<p class=\"western\">Switch back to <strong>RGB<\/strong> at any time to return to the full screen-accurate colour display. Your selected colour model is saved into the <span style=\"font-family: Courier New, monospace;\">.opub<\/span> file and persists between sessions.<\/p>\n<h3 class=\"western\"><span style=\"color: #007670;\">When to Use It<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignright wp-image-1925\" src=\"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/WhenToUseCMYK.webp\" alt=\"When to Use CMYK Mode\" width=\"128\" height=\"110\" srcset=\"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/WhenToUseCMYK.webp 421w, http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/08\/WhenToUseCMYK-300x259.webp 300w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 128px) 100vw, 128px\" \/><\/span><\/h3>\n<p class=\"western\">CMYK mode is most useful when:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-size: small;\">Designing flyers, brochures, or any document that will be sent to a commercial print shop.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/li>\n<li>\n<p align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-size: small;\">Working with bright or vivid brand colours that may not survive the RGB-to-CMYK conversion faithfully.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/li>\n<li>\n<p align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-size: small;\">Checking whether a full-bleed background or image will retain its intended appearance in physical print.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>CMYK Commercial Print Mode Professional printing services work in the CMYK colour model (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow, Key\/Black) rather than the RGB model used by screens. CMYK has a smaller colour gamut &#8211; it cannot reproduce certain vivid neon or electric colours that look brilliant on screen. A design that appears vibrant on your monitor can [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1927,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","template":"","knowledgebase_cat":[23],"class_list":["post-1916","knowledgebase","type-knowledgebase","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","knowledgebase_cat-accessibility"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/knowledgebase\/1916","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/knowledgebase"}],"about":[{"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/knowledgebase"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/1"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=1916"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/knowledgebase\/1916\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1929,"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/knowledgebase\/1916\/revisions\/1929"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1927"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1916"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"knowledgebase_cat","embeddable":true,"href":"http:\/\/acr.floydcraft.co.uk\/openpublisher\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/knowledgebase_cat?post=1916"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}